{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/app carries the resident ownership claim, at head 36ef76f10abc9631657ff6dd61a663348531ae88 on request/multi-resident-coordination, cut from main 84de35f6.\n\nResidentRef(genesis) names refs/gitseq/resident/\u003cgenesis\u003e. ClaimResident acquires, contests, takes over and refuses; ResidentOwnership.Release withdraws. Acquisition, takeover and release are one primitive, a Git ref update carrying the expected old value, so exactly one process holds the claim at a time. Each claim blob carries a fresh sixteen-byte random nonce, generated on every acquisition attempt including retries, which makes the object id unique to one acquisition and closes A-B-A. Reads are bounded at 8 KiB and contention at eight attempts; an unreadable, malformed, incomplete, or foreign claim is a refusal that names the ref and the recovery command, never a vacancy. Only Dead authorizes a takeover; Liveness's zero value is Ambiguous, so a prober that decides nothing refuses. PublishResident and ResidentURL keep their signatures and meaning and are now documented as endpoint metadata rather than authority.\n\nTests at this head, in internal/app/resident_test.go, each pinning one interleaving with an injected prober so the ordering is exact rather than timed: twelve simultaneous starters yield exactly one owner and eleven refusals; a second starter refuses and names the incumbent; six shapes of unreadable claim (not JSON, truncated, empty, no nonce, oversized, another workroom) are each refused under the most permissive prober available and leave the ref untouched; a stale claim is taken over after a definitive negative; an ambiguous probe leaves the claim alone; eight takers racing one stale claim yield one owner; a delayed taker whose observed claim is displaced mid-probe by a full release-and-reacquire refuses and leaves the new owner's claim in place; a taker and a clean shutdown yield one owner in both orders; every acquisition carries a fresh 32-hex nonce across five acquisitions; and a symlinked path and a linked worktree both contend for the one claim while a separate repository serves concurrently.\n\nThe delayed-taker test is mutation-sensitive, checked rather than asserted: replacing the conditional swap with an unconditional update-ref failed it, along with the simultaneous-starters and two-takers tests, and the mutation was reverted.\n\nGates at this exact head: go vet ./... clean; go test -count=1 ./... exit 0, 25 packages ok. The durable CAS path is untouched — the kernel concurrency tests pass unchanged, and this ref is a separate namespace beside refs/gitseq/checkpoints.\n\nGoverned by request 78963842b2a9e67962d350db9a4d7bf72a07fe85 and direction proposal v3 998918b6a065170ab1558f6a1d1d37dc12477aa6, approved by codex in eb555d7eed99c8275b91cdf2224d6c0e1942cc01.","body":{"commit":"36ef76f10abc9631657ff6dd61a663348531ae88","path":"internal/app"}}